{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://crucible.local/contracts/review-category.schema.json",
  "title": "Review Category",
  "description": "Every category a reviewer may give a finding or a speculative entry: the union of the reviewer vocabularies (each reviewer agent's Category Vocabulary section, listed per reviewer in x-reviewers). x-categories flags each category: security true puts an item in it under the security floor (review-core, Realism test) — set aside only with proof, never unproven; an item from lens-security-reviewer is under the floor whatever its category. The flow-review writers reject a category outside the reviewer's own list in x-reviewers, and one outside this set for a reviewer x-reviewers does not list. x-security-terms lists unambiguous names of security weaknesses, and x-security-patterns the word sets of the weakness patterns below: a finding whose problem or trigger, or a speculative entry whose concern, names a weakness is a security concern, so the writers reject it under a category that is not a security one and the reviewer re-files it under one of its own security categories or, if none fits, passes it to lens-security-reviewer as a handoff (as a reviewer with none always does). Text is read case-insensitively in sentences (split at . ; : ! ? and line breaks) of clauses (split further at , and a closing parenthesis) of whole words: text inside a backtick code span is skipped except for a ../ or ..\\ or an == or != in it, any run of characters other than a letter, a digit or an underscore separates words (sql-injection matches sql injection), and an identifier holding an underscore is one word (csrf_token never matches csrf). A word set gives words, prefixes (a word starting with one counts) and phrases. A clause names a weakness by: (1) a term, as consecutive words, its last word with an optional plural s, unless a word of x-security-mechanism-words (or its plural) follows it directly (csrf token check, sql injection prevention) and the clause holds no word of x-security-negation-words outside the term and that mechanism word (missing csrf protection, xss filter can be bypassed: then it still names the weakness); (2) the word injection within 3 words, on either side, of a word of x-injection-kinds (injection (SQL)), under the same mechanism rule, never when the word before it is one of x-dependency-injection-words (constructor injection names a design pattern); (3) a subject of x-access-subjects and a word starting with one of x-bypass-prefixes anywhere in the clause (the authorization check can easily be bypassed), under the same mechanism rule; (4) such a subject followed directly by a mechanism word, with a negation word in the clause (no authorization check); neither (3) nor (4) when the words from the clause-subordinators word before the match to the one after it hold a performance-objects word and the clause holds no access-loss word (skip the redundant permission lookup, the auth cache bypasses the LRU eviction, but a password check skipped when the cache is warm still counts); (5) an unauthenticated word with a reach word (unauthenticated callers can reach the endpoint); (6) a ../ or ..\\ with a traversal-escape word, or an unnormalized word with a path-nouns word; (7) a concatenation word followed by into and, within 3 words, an injection-sinks word, or an unescaped word with an injection-sinks word (the term is concatenated into the SQL string); (8) a secrets word with a comparison word and either an == or != or the words constant time with a not-constant-time word (the token compare uses ==); (9) a fail-open-as word followed by as, or a fail-open-to word followed by to, and within 2 words an access-grants word (the request is treated as authenticated); (10) a revocation word, then a retention word, then an access-nouns word (a revoked user keeps admin access). A sentence names one when only is followed within 2 words by a check-verbs word and later by an authentication-states word, and a negation word other than only is followed in its clause by an authorization-objects word (only checks that the user is logged in, not the admin role). An item from lens-test-quality-reviewer whose text names a subject of x-test-security-subjects or a term (matched the same way, a mechanism word after it included) is refused under a category that is not a security one too: it is filed as untested-security. The flow-testing test-plan writers refuse a not_tested entry whose what, reason or accepting names a weakness, a term or such a subject unless its category is security or authentication, which outranks every other required category. flow-review/scripts/lib/review-categories.json is a byte-identical copy of this file the scripts read, with lib/security-terms.jq, the matcher; flow-testing's scripts read both from the sibling flow-review skill.",
